---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const enable = `import { createMdbase } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";
import manifest from "./mdbase-app.json";

export const mdbase = createMdbase(
  { id: manifest.id, name: manifest.name, origin: location.origin, environment: "production", manifest },
  { unsynced: true },
);`;

const render = `function render(snapshot) {
  const { phase } = snapshot;
  if (phase.kind === "ready" || phase.kind === "unsynced") {
    showApp(phase.connection.records);
    savedOnlyHere.hidden = phase.kind !== "unsynced" || !snapshot.unsyncedWork;
  }
}

// "Connect to sync your work"
connectButton.onclick = () => mdbase.signIn();

// On a sign-in screen: "Continue without connecting"
continueButton.onclick = () => mdbase.unsynced.open();`;

const transfer = `// A collection is open (phase "ready") and snapshot.unsyncedWork is set.
const plan = await mdbase.unsynced.planTransfer();

// plan.counts: { create, present, skipped, renamed, changed }
// plan.missingTypes: types the records use that this collection lacks
if (plan.missingTypes.length > 0 && (await userAgreesToAddTypes(plan.missingTypes))) {
  return bringIn(await mdbase.unsynced.planTransfer({ addTypes: true }));
}
await bringIn(plan);

async function bringIn(plan) {
  const result = await mdbase.unsynced.transfer(plan, {
    onProgress: ({ done, total }) => showProgress(done, total),
  });
  if (result.failed.length > 0) {
    // Plan and transfer again to retry. Nothing already done repeats.
  }
}`;

const map = `const plan = await mdbase.unsynced.planTransfer({
  map: (record) => {
    if (!record.types.includes("worklog-entry")) return null; // leave it out
    // Each record arrives with its body and complete source.
    return { path: \`journal/\${record.path}\`, document: record.document };
  },
});`;

const discard = `if (await confirm("Delete the work saved in this browser? This cannot be undone.")) {
  await mdbase.unsynced.discard();
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Work before connecting"
  description="Let people use your app before they sign in. Their work is kept in the browser and brought into a collection when they connect one."
  active="unsynced"
  headings={[
    { href: "#what", label: "What it is" },
    { href: "#enable", label: "Turn it on" },
    { href: "#render", label: "Show it" },
    { href: "#transfer", label: "Bring the work in" },
    { href: "#map", label: "Reshape records" },
    { href: "#discard", label: "Discard" },
    { href: "#limits", label: "Limits" }
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p>
    With the <code>unsynced</code> option, a signed-out visitor gets a
    working app straight away. The SDK opens an <strong>unsynced
    collection</strong>: a real collection, with your types installed, stored
    in this browser and nowhere else. Your code reads and writes it through
    the same <code>MdbaseClient</code> as a synced one.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the person later signs in and opens a collection, the work they did
    can be brought into it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/sdk/ui/">shared UI</a> handles all of this when you pass
    {" "}<code>unsynced</code> to <code>CollectionGate</code> and
    {" "}<code>CollectionSwitcher</code>. The rest of this page is for apps
    that draw it themselves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="enable">Turn it on</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={enable} language="ts" label="mdbase.ts" />
  <p>
    With the option set, <code>start()</code> opens the unsynced collection
    whenever the person is signed out and the URL names no collection. The
    phase is <code>unsynced</code>. The types in your manifest’s
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/manifest/#provisioning">provisions</a> are installed
    in it automatically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="render">Show it</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={render} language="ts" label="main.ts" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Treat <code>unsynced</code> like <code>ready</code>:
      {" "}<code>phase.connection</code> is the same kind of connection.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tell the person where their work is. Once something has been saved,
      {" "}<code>snapshot.unsyncedWork</code> is set; show a line such as
      “Saved only in this browser” with a way to connect.
    </li>
    <li>
      The unsynced collection is never listed in
      {" "}<code>snapshot.collections</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A signed-in person with no collection open is in the
      {" "}<code>choosing</code> phase as usual.
      {" "}<code>mdbase.unsynced.open()</code> opens the unsynced collection
      from there, or from a sign-in screen that offers “Continue without
      connecting”.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="transfer">Bring the work in</h2>
  <p>
    After the person connects, <code>snapshot.unsyncedWork</code> stays set
    while the collection they opened does not have the work yet. Plan the
    transfer, show what it would do when there is something to decide, then
    run it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={transfer} language="ts" label="bring-in.ts" />
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Item outcome</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>create</code></td><td>The record will be created, with the same ID.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>present</code></td><td>An earlier transfer already brought it in.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>skipped</code></td><td>Your <code>map</code> left it out.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>changed</code></td><td>It was brought in earlier and has changed here since. The newer version is created beside the first as a copy.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Planning writes nothing. A plan is safe to throw away.
    </li>
    <li>
      A transfer can be interrupted by a reload or a lost connection. Plan
      and transfer again: only what is missing is created.
    </li>
    <li>
      When a path is already taken in the destination, the record goes to a
      free path beside it; the item’s <code>renamedFrom</code> has the path
      you asked for.
    </li>
    <li>
      The destination’s own types and settings are never replaced. With
      {" "}<code>addTypes</code>, types it lacks are added from the unsynced
      collection.
    </li>
    <li>
      When everything is in the destination, <code>result.retired</code> is
      true and the unsynced collection starts again empty.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="map">Reshape records</h2>
  <p>
    By default each record keeps its path and its whole Markdown source. Pass
    {" "}<code>map</code> to change where records go, or to leave some out:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={map} language="ts" label="bring-in.ts" />
  <p>
    The function returns a <code>CreateInput</code>, or <code>null</code> to
    leave the record out. A record always keeps its ID; returning a different
    {" "}<code>id</code> is refused.
  </p>

  <h2 id="discard">Discard</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={discard} language="ts" label="discard.ts" />
  <p>
    Discarding erases the unsynced collection from this browser. Ask first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The work is in browser storage. Clearing site data, or a browser that
      evicts storage under pressure, removes it. Say so in your interface, and
      offer connecting early.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is per browser and per origin. It is not on the person’s other
      devices, and a development build on another port has its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      There is one unsynced collection per app in a browser.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
